From: Jeremy Bettcher <thebettcher@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] PPC64: save the caller's CR in the caller's CR save word, not 8(sp)

Portability fix, upstreamable: this is a straight ELFv2 ABI conformance bug
in the ppc64le interpreter, not a packaging workaround.

`saveregs` pushed the interpreter frame with

    stdu sp, -CFRAME_SPACE(sp)

and then saved the caller's CR with

    std r0, SAVE_CR        // SAVE_CR = 8(sp)

8(sp) and 16(sp) of a frame are the ELFv2 linkage-area CR and LR save words
that the frame owner's *callees* write into -- a C prologue does
`stw r12,8(r1)` and `std r0,16(r1)` against its caller's sp, which is ours.
So every C helper the interpreter calls that itself uses a non-volatile CR
field (CR2-CR4) overwrote the interpreter's saved CR, and `restoreregs` then
handed the C caller back a corrupted CR2/CR3/CR4.

SAVE_LR already avoided this by using CFRAME_SPACE+16 (the caller's LR save
word, which we own as a callee).  SAVE_CR now uses CFRAME_SPACE+8 the same
way.

Observed as: neovim aborts on

    nvim --headless -u NONE -i NONE -c 'checkhealth vim.health' -c 'qa!'

with "try_leave: Assertion `trylevel > 0' failed", because GCC caches
do_cmdline()'s `flags & DOCMD_EXCRESET` predicate in CR3 across thousands of
instructions and many calls, and the FileType autocommand runs Lua in
between.  The same neovim source built against PUC Lua 5.1 does not abort.
Reproduced directly with a C probe: Lua string concatenation and
string.format/gsub both returned to C with CR2 and CR3 zeroed; every other
Lua operation tested was clean.

diff --git a/src/vm_ppc64.dasc b/src/vm_ppc64.dasc
index b9184091..66e7ba5b 100644
--- a/src/vm_ppc64.dasc
+++ b/src/vm_ppc64.dasc
@@ -127,6 +127,16 @@
 |// otherwise destroy the C frame record.  Hence the C frame info and the two
 |// conversion temps sit ABOVE the register saves, not in 32..95.
 |.define SAVE_LR,	480(sp)
+|// ELFv2: 8(sp) and 16(sp) of *our own* frame are the CR and LR save words
+|// that *our callees* write into (a C helper prologue does a stw of r12 to
+|// 8 of r1 and a std of r0 to 16 of r1 against the caller's sp, which is
+|// ours -- ELFv2 linkage area).  The slots
+|// we own are the matching ones in our caller's frame, at CFRAME_SPACE+8
+|// and CFRAME_SPACE+16.  SAVE_LR already uses CFRAME_SPACE+16; SAVE_CR must
+|// use CFRAME_SPACE+8, not 8(sp), or every C helper that touches a
+|// non-volatile CR field (CR2-CR4) overwrites our saved CR and restoreregs
+|// hands the caller back a corrupted CR.
+|.define SAVE_CR,	472(sp)	// 64 bit CR save (caller's CR save word).
 |.define CFRAME_SPACE,	464	// Delta for sp.
 |// Back chain for sp:	464(sp)	<-- sp entering interpreter
 |.define SAVE_ERRF,	456(sp)	// |
@@ -144,9 +154,9 @@
 |//			96(sp)	// .. 111: free = JIT spill slots 24..27 (SPS_FIXED).
 |//			32(sp)	// .. 95: callee parameter save area (ABI mandated);
 |//				// callee-scratch, keep nothing here.
-|.define SAVE_TOC,	24(sp)	// TOC save area.
-|// Next frame lr:	16(sp)
-|.define SAVE_CR,	8(sp)	// 64 bit CR save.
+|.define SAVE_TOC,	24(sp)	// TOC save area (ours: we are the caller).
+|// Next frame lr:	16(sp)	// written by our callees -- do not use.
+|// Next frame cr:	8(sp)	// written by our callees -- do not use.
 |// Back chain for sp:	0(sp)   <-- sp while in interpreter
 |
 |.define TMPD_BLO,	408(sp)	// LSB
